## Why This Is Asked
Aims to gauge handling of ambiguous prompts with simple, maintainable logic—crucial for multi-tenant data workflows without heavy ML.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight keyword-based intent detection
- Ambiguity handling with clarifying questions
- Structured 2-step plans and module payloads
- Lightweight testing with sample prompts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo JS prototype for disambiguation harness
function detectIntents(text){
  const intents = {
    time: /(last|recent|past|last month)/i,
    region: /(region|country|geo)/i,
    users: /(users|accounts|people)/i
  };
  const hits = Object.keys(intents).filter(k => intents[k].test(text));
  return hits;
}

function disambiguate(text){
  const intents = detectIntents(text);
  if (intents.length > 1){
    return {ambiguous: true, options: ["summarize by region", "summarize by time"]};
  }
  if (intents.length === 1){
    return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Fetch data", "Summarize"], payload: {module: intents[0]}};
  }
  return {ambiguous: false, plan: ["Interpret", "Respond"], payload: {module: 'default'}};
}

console.log(disambiguate("summarize recent activity"));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the heuristic to handle multilingual prompts?
- What tests would you add to prevent regression when adding new intents?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests ability to design auditable provenance in high-velocity multi-tenant prompts, focusing on traceability, tamper-evidence, and policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt provenance
- Per-step hashing and transformation tracing
- Tamper-evident signing
- Multitenant policy binding and data classifications

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal JS prototype for provenance chain
const crypto = require('crypto');
function hash(s){ return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(s).digest('hex'); }
function provenance(rawPrompt, policy){
  const steps = [];
  steps.push({step:'raw', hash: hash(rawPrompt)});
  const guarded = rawPrompt + " /* guarded */";
  steps.push({step:'guard', description:'guard injection'});
  const finalPrompt = guarded;
  steps.push({step:'policy', description:'enforcement'});
  return {finalPrompt, provenance_id: 'p'+Date.now(), steps};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this across thousands of tenants while preserving auditability?
- What storage design would you choose for provenance data and how would you query it for audits?

